window.FOVAL_COURSES = [{"id":"bible-basics","title":"The Bible: What It Is and How to Read It","school":"christian-studies","subject":"Christian Studies","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"What the Bible is, how it is put together, where it came from, and how to read each kind of writing in it well. For anyone new to it or coming back.","description":"The Bible is the most read and most misread book in the world, and most people who own one were never shown how it works. This course starts at the beginning: the sixty-six books and why Catholic and Orthodox Bibles have more; the two testaments and the one story that runs from Genesis to Revelation; the different kinds of writing inside (story, law, poetry, wisdom, prophecy, gospel, letter, apocalyptic) and the rules for reading each one; how the collection formed, how the text was copied and what we can say about its reliability; how translations differ and how to choose one; and a way of reading that you can keep up. It is taught from within the Christian faith and says so. It also says plainly what history establishes, what scholars argue about, and what Christians hold by faith, and it presents the views it disagrees with in their own terms.","outcomes":["Find any book in the Bible, say which section and genre it belongs to, explain why the Jewish and Christian orders differ, and place it on the timeline of the one story from creation to new creation","Identify the kind of writing in a passage and apply the reading rules for that kind, so that a story, a law, a psalm, a proverb, a prophecy, a parable, a letter, and a vision are each read as what they are and not as something else","Explain the Old Testament law and the prophets as one covenant made and enforced, and compare the main Christian ways of reading them now in each view's own terms","Explain, with dated evidence, how the canon formed, why Protestant, Catholic, and Orthodox Bibles differ, and what the Council of Nicaea did not do, giving each tradition's case as it would give it","Describe how the text was copied and recovered, what the Dead Sea Scrolls and the Greek manuscripts show, read the three famous disputed passages in your own Bible, and state fairly what is established and what is still argued about reliability","Sort any claim about the Bible as established by history, argued from evidence, or held by faith, and describe the inerrantist, Catholic, Orthodox, mainline, and critical-scholarly views of Scripture so their holders would recognise them","Compare formal, functional, and free translations, explain why the KJV differs from modern versions, and choose a translation for reading and one for study with reasons you can defend","Read a whole book of the Bible with a repeatable method and a plan you can keep, and write a short reading guide to it for someone else"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":true,"standpoint":"christian","hours":33.3,"lessons":[{"id":"01-finding-your-way-around","title":"Finding your way around: what the Bible is and how it is laid out","minutes":70,"objectives":["Identify the section and the kind of writing of any book of the Bible from its position alone","Explain why the Christian Old Testament and the Jewish Tanakh hold the same books in a different order, and what each order's last word does to the reader","Explain what chapter and verse numbers are, when they were added, and what they are not","Sort a claim about the Bible as something history establishes, something scholars argue about, or something this course holds by faith"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"02-one-story","title":"One story: Genesis to Revelation in a single sitting","minutes":135,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/christian-studies/bible-basics/02-one-story.mp3","objectives":["Place any book of the Bible on the timeline of the one story, in the right act and on the right side of the four-hundred-year gap","Explain the five covenants (Noah, Abraham, Sinai, David, the new covenant) as the spine of the story and say what each one promises","Apply Fee and Stuart's three levels of narrative to a passage and state what it is doing at each level","Explain why a passage's place in the story changes what it can mean, using a real passage and its original audience"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"03-genre-first-and-narrative","title":"Genre first: what kind of writing is this, and how to read a story","minutes":150,"objectives":["Explain why identifying the kind of writing is the first interpretive decision, and use Fee and Stuart's basic rule as a boundary test on a passage","Apply the ten principles for Old Testament narrative to a story, reading it on all three levels","Distinguish what a narrative describes from what it prescribes, and tell the places where the narrator does evaluate from the places where he does not","Identify when a narrative's silence is being read as approval, and say where the evaluation should come from instead"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"04-law-and-prophets","title":"Covenant made, covenant enforced: reading the law and the prophets","minutes":145,"objectives":["Explain what the Old Testament law is (the terms of the Sinai covenant, written in the shape of an ancient treaty) and compare the main Christian proposals for how it applies now, in each proposal's own terms","State the main frameworks for relating the Old Testament to the New (covenant theology, dispensationalism, and the positions between) in terms their holders would accept, and say what reading habits each one produces","Apply the near-horizon and far-horizon reading to a prophetic oracle the lesson has not worked, and say what its first hearers would have heard","Identify a \"prophecy means prediction\" error in a reading and correct it with Deuteronomy 28 open","State the Jewish reading of Isaiah 7:14 and Isaiah 53 in terms a Jewish reader would accept, state the Christian reading in its own terms, and sort each part of the dispute as established, argued, or held by faith"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"05-poetry-psalms-wisdom","title":"Reading poetry, the Psalms, and wisdom","minutes":145,"objectives":["Identify parallelism and imagery in a Hebrew poem and explain what the second line adds to the first","Classify a psalm by type using the shape of its parts, find the turn in a lament, and read the psalm by its movement rather than verse by verse","Explain why a proverb is not a promise, and apply that rule to a proverb the lesson did not cover","Explain how Job and Ecclesiastes work as counter-voices inside the same canon as Proverbs, and say who is speaking before you take any speech in them as teaching","Read an imprecatory psalm as the prayer it is, state the strongest objection to praying it, and say how Christians who pray the Psalter daily have answered that objection differently"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"06-gospels-parables-acts","title":"Reading the Gospels, the parables, and Acts","minutes":140,"objectives":["Explain why there are four Gospels, and read one event across the parallel accounts without forcing them into one","Apply the one-point-with-a-punch rule to a parable and identify allegorising when you see it","Distinguish what Acts describes from what it prescribes, using a test you can state and knowing what it cannot settle","State the conservative and the critical dating of the Gospels in each side's own terms, and say what both agree on","State the case for and against the traditional authorship of the Gospels as each side puts it, and sort the pieces as established, argued, or held by faith"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"07-letters-and-revelation","title":"Reading the letters and Revelation","minutes":130,"objectives":["Reconstruct the situation a letter answers, reading by paragraph rather than by verse","Apply the basic rule and the comparable-particulars test to decide what a first-century instruction asks of a reader now","Explain how apocalyptic works, in terms of symbol, Old Testament allusion, and vision as picture","Read a passage of Revelation by those conventions, tracing its images to the Old Testament passages they come from","State the preterist, historicist, futurist, and idealist readings in terms their holders would recognise","Say what the manuscript evidence and the earliest commentator settle about Revelation 13:18, and what the book's date settles for the preterist reading"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"08-how-the-canon-formed","title":"How the canon formed, and why Catholic and Orthodox Bibles have more books","minutes":130,"objectives":["Explain how the Jewish canon reached the shape Christians inherited, and say which parts of that story are settled and which are argued","Walk the dated witnesses for the New Testament canon in order, and say what each one shows","Apply the three tests the church used to a book this lesson does not settle for you","Give the Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant cases for the Old Testament's extent, each in terms its own members would accept","Compare the three accounts of what a canon is, and say what they agree on","Sort a claim about the canon as established by history, argued from evidence, or held by faith"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"09-text-and-reliability","title":"How the text reached us, and how reliable it is","minutes":140,"objectives":["Describe how a hand-copied text is reconstructed from many copies, and why age, geographical spread, and the harder reading each count as evidence","State what the Great Isaiah Scroll and the fourth-century Greek codices each showed, and what each did not show","Explain what a textual variant is, roughly how many there are, what kinds most of them are, and why the count on its own tells you almost nothing",{"Work an unfamiliar disputed passage":"read the note in a modern Bible, find the evidence behind it, and say what it rests on"},"Sort a claim about the reliability of the text as established by history, argued among scholars, or held by faith"],"quizCount":8},{"id":"10-what-we-believe","title":"What we believe about this book, and what others make of it","minutes":155,"objectives":["Explain inspiration, inerrancy, infallibility, and authority as the Christian tradition uses the words, and compare the main positions within Christianity in terms their holders would accept","Describe the critical-scholarly view of the Bible so that a student of Hayes or Martin would recognise it","Sort a claim about the Bible as established by history, argued from evidence, or held by faith, and say what would change its category","Identify what Christians of every position share, and where their disagreement actually lies"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"11-choosing-a-translation","title":"Choosing a translation","minutes":155,"objectives":["Explain what source texts modern translations are made from and why the King James Version differs from them at passages like 1 John 5:7","Compare formal, functional, and free translation and place the major English versions on the spectrum with reasons","Explain the gender-language question in the terms each publisher uses for its own decision","Choose a translation for reading and one for study, and defend each choice with a trade-off rather than a slogan"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"12-method-tools-plan","title":"A way of reading you can keep: method, tools, and a plan","minutes":150,"objectives":["Apply the four questions (what kind of writing, where in the story, what it meant then, what it means now) to a whole short book from a cold start","Use context in widening circles (sentence, paragraph, book, testament, Bible) to check a reading of a single verse","Identify which free tool answers which question about a passage, and say which questions this course leaves to the next one","Build a fourteen-day reading plan with a log, and explain why a small daily reading on a schedule beats a large one once"],"quizCount":6}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":55,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":22},{"id":"project-read-one-book","title":"Read one book by its genre, over two weeks, and write its guide","type":"project","minutes":300,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/bible-basics.js?v=b1effd07"},{"id":"clear-writing","title":"Writing Clearly","school":"communication-and-people","subject":"Communication & People","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"Write so that people understand you the first time. Built on what research says readers actually do with a sentence, not on a list of rules you were handed at school.","description":"Clear writing is not a matter of taste and it is not politeness. It is the difference between being understood and not. This course teaches the reader-expectation tradition: where a reader looks for the point, which word they will emphasise, what happens when a subject and its verb drift apart, and why an action buried in a noun is hard work to read. It covers the sentence, the paragraph and the whole document, it is honest about where the famous rules came from and which of them survive contact with evidence, and it ends with the step almost no writing course teaches: finding out from a real reader whether any of it worked.","outcomes":["Say what a reader is likely to take from a page, and write for that","Put the point where the reader is looking for it, and know when not to","Make the character of a sentence its subject and the action its verb","Use the topic and stress positions to control what a reader takes away","Cut a document by a third by naming the kind of every cut you make","Structure a document so that a skimming reader can navigate it","Test a writing rule against evidence instead of accepting it","Run a paraphrase test on your own writing and act on what it shows"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":false,"hours":14.5,"lessons":[{"id":"01-what-happens-when-someone-reads-you","title":"What actually happens when someone reads you","minutes":65,"objectives":["State roughly how much of a page a reader has time for, and say where that number comes from and where it does not apply","Identify who a particular document is really for, and what they came to it needing","Explain why a text's difficulty depends on who is reading it, and what a readability score can and cannot tell you"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"02-put-the-point-where-the-reader-is-looking","title":"Put the point where the reader is looking","minutes":75,"objectives":["Front-load a document, a section and a paragraph, and say what each level of front-loading is doing","Rewrite an email whose decision is buried so the decision arrives first, without losing the reasons","Explain why context has to come before the thing it contextualises, and name a case where the point should not come first"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"03-characters-and-actions","title":"Characters and actions","minutes":75,"objectives":["Name the real character and the real action in a sentence, before rewriting anything","Rewrite a sentence so the character is its subject and the action is its verb","Recognise a nominalisation, and decide case by case whether undoing it helps or hurts"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"04-keep-the-subject-next-to-the-verb","title":"Keep the subject next to the verb","minutes":75,"objectives":["Explain what a reader is doing while a subject waits for its verb, and why the cost rises with every word","Measure the gap between subject and verb in your own sentences, and close the worst ones three different ways","Decide when an interruption earns its place, and say what a long readable sentence is doing differently"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"05-topic-and-stress-position","title":"Topic position and stress position","minutes":80,"objectives":["Identify the topic position and the stress position of any sentence, and say what a reader does with each","Repair a paragraph that reads as a list of unrelated facts, by moving old information to the front of each sentence","Explain why the passive is sometimes the correct choice, using the topic position rather than a rule"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"06-concision-that-is-teachable","title":"Concision that is actually teachable","minutes":75,"objectives":["Name the kind of every cut you make, using a fixed list rather than an impression","Cut a passage by a third without losing a fact, and say what each cut was","Explain why omit needless words fails as instruction, and identify repetition that must be kept"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"07-shape-at-the-document-level","title":"Shape at the document level","minutes":45,"objectives":["Write headings that let a reader decide whether to stop, rather than naming a category","Choose between prose, a list and a table by asking what relationship the content has","Restructure a document without rewriting it, and test the result using its headings alone"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"08-rules-and-where-they-come-from","title":"Rules, and where they come from","minutes":55,"objectives":["Tell a rule that describes how readers process prose from one that asserts a prohibition","Identify a passive clause, and say when it is the right choice rather than a fault","Test a usage rule against evidence yourself, using a corpus and a count"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"09-revising-and-testing-it","title":"Revising, and finding out whether it worked","minutes":50,"objectives":["Separate revising from editing, and say what each one is for and when it happens","Run a paraphrase test on a page of your own writing with a real reader","Say what a readability score does and does not tell you about whether you were understood"],"quizCount":6}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":35,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":20},{"id":"project-one-document","title":"Take one real document all the way","type":"project","minutes":240,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/clear-writing.js?v=219d5b62"},{"id":"python-basics","title":"Introduction to Programming with Python","school":"computer-science-and-ai","subject":"Computer Science & AI","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"Learn to think like a programmer and write real Python from your first lesson. No prior experience needed.","description":"Programming is the skill of giving precise instructions to a machine. This course teaches you the core ideas every programmer relies on, using Python, one of the most readable and widely used languages in the world. By the end you will be able to write small programs that store data, make decisions, repeat work, and organise themselves into reusable pieces. Every lesson also teaches you to read one more kind of error message, because a beginner meets their first one about ninety seconds in, and reading them is a skill rather than a talent.","outcomes":["Run Python two ways, and read a traceback from the bottom up to find what stopped and why","Explain what = does in terms of names and values, and convert what input() gives you","Choose between branches with if, elif and else, and predict which one runs","Repeat work with for and while, and build an accumulator that totals and counts","Write functions with parameters and return values, and say what return gives that print does not","Use lists and dictionaries, and predict what happens when two names share one list"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":false,"hours":9.7,"lessons":[{"id":"01-first-program","title":"Your first program, and your first error","minutes":55,"objectives":["Run a Python program two ways, in the interpreter and from a saved file","Explain what each line of a traceback tells you, reading from the bottom up","Identify a SyntaxError and a NameError from the message alone","Apply the edit, run, read loop to fix a program that is broken three different ways"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"02-variables","title":"Names and values","minutes":60,"objectives":["Explain what the equals sign does, in terms of names and values rather than equality","Predict what each name is worth after a sequence of assignments","Identify the type of a value, and explain why \"2\" + \"2\" is \"22\"","Apply int() and float() to convert what input() hands back"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"03-conditions","title":"Making decisions","minutes":60,"objectives":["Explain the difference between = and == and recognise what Python says when you confuse them","Predict which branch of an if, elif, else chain runs for a given input","Apply and, or and not to combine conditions, and explain what short-circuiting protects you from","Identify which values Python treats as false without a comparison"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"04-loops","title":"Repeating work","minutes":65,"objectives":["Compare for and while, and explain which one a given job needs","Build an accumulator loop that totals and counts, and explain why it starts outside the loop","Predict the output of a loop over range(), including one with a step","Identify an off-by-one error and an unguarded empty case in someone else's loop"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"05-functions","title":"Functions","minutes":85,"objectives":["Explain the difference between returning a value and printing one, and say what each is for","Build a function with parameters, a default and a return value, and call it by keyword","Predict what a name is worth inside a function and after it has finished","Apply a function to remove repetition from a program that has three copies of one block"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"06-collections","title":"Lists and dictionaries","minutes":95,"objectives":["Build and index a list, and explain why the first item sits at position 0","Apply a dictionary to look something up by name rather than by position","Explain aliasing, and predict what b = a followed by a change through b does to a","Identify an IndexError and a KeyError from the message and name the cause of each"],"quizCount":6}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":40,"pass_mark":0.7,"quizCount":15},{"id":"project-readings-summary","title":"The readings summariser","type":"project","minutes":120,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/python-basics.js?v=9fd6f23e"},{"id":"logic-and-argument","title":"Logic and Argument","school":"foundations","subject":"Foundations","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"How to tell a good argument from a bad one, take apart the arguments you meet, and build ones that hold up.","description":"Most arguments you meet, in the news, at work, and in your own head, are never checked. This course gives you the tools to check them. You will learn what makes a deductive argument valid and sound, what makes an inductive argument strong, and how probability decides how much a piece of evidence should move you. You will practise reconstructing what someone actually argued, putting it in its strongest form before you answer it, and finding the missing step. You will learn the standard fallacies by their real names and real examples, and also why the experts who study them warn against using the names as verdicts. The course draws on the textbooks universities assign, the primary texts from Aristotle to Mill and Toulmin, and the psychology of how people actually reason, and it ends with you writing an argument of your own.","outcomes":["Identify the premises, conclusion, and missing premise of an argument, put it in standard form, and tell an argument from an explanation, a report, or a bare assertion","Judge whether a deductive argument is valid and sound by finding a counterexample, using the four conditional forms and a Venn check, and test the link before consulting your opinion of the conclusion","Judge whether an inductive argument is strong and cogent, whether it generalises from a sample, argues by analogy, claims a cause, or infers the best explanation","Use natural frequencies, the conjunction rule, base rates, and the likelihood ratio to work out how much a piece of evidence should change your mind, and spot the prosecutor's fallacy","Reconstruct someone else's argument in its strongest form, with Toulmin's layout and Rapoport's rules, and evaluate it with the acceptability, relevance, and grounds conditions","Name the standard fallacies, recognise them in documented real cases, and use critical questions to tell a fallacy from the legitimate argument that shares its shape","Check a news argument for source quality, relative versus absolute risk, survivorship, and your own motivated reasoning, with a repeatable checklist","Build a one-page written argument that states its strongest objection, concedes or answers it, and survives your own fallacy check and a reader's"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":false,"hours":19.5,"lessons":[{"id":"01-what-an-argument-is","title":"What an argument is, and how to find the missing step","minutes":85,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/foundations/logic-and-argument/01-what-an-argument-is.mp3","objectives":["Identify the conclusion and premises of a passage and rewrite it in standard form","Distinguish an argument from an explanation, a report, a bare assertion, and a lone conditional","Supply the most plausible missing premise of an argument, in the form the arguer would accept","Explain why the unstated premise of a real argument is where its weak step hides when there is one, and why you can't tell until it's written down"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"02-valid-and-sound","title":"Valid, sound, and why your opinion of the conclusion gets in the way","minutes":70,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/foundations/logic-and-argument/02-valid-and-sound.mp3","objectives":["Explain validity as the absence of any possible case in which the premises are true and the conclusion false","Show an argument is invalid by giving a counterexample, a possible case with true premises and a false conclusion, found directly or by swapping in parallel content","Compare valid and invalid arguments with true and false premises, and identify the one combination that cannot occur","Predict how belief bias will affect a judgement of validity, and apply the fix of testing the link before consulting your opinion"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"03-conditionals","title":"Conditionals: the four forms and the two that fool almost everyone","minutes":95,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/foundations/logic-and-argument/03-conditionals.mp3","objectives":["Identify modus ponens, modus tollens, hypothetical syllogism, and disjunctive syllogism in everyday arguments","Identify affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent and show why each is invalid with a counterexample","Translate \"only if\", \"unless\", and necessary and sufficient statements into \"if, then\" form correctly","Explain what the Wason selection task shows about how people test a conditional, and what changes it"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"04-syllogisms","title":"All, no, some: checking a syllogism in two minutes","minutes":60,"objectives":["Translate everyday sentences into the four categorical forms (all, no, some, some not) and state the exact sentence that contradicts each one","Test a two-premise syllogism for validity with a three-circle Venn diagram and name the counterexample when it fails","Explain why \"some A are B\" does not mean \"some A are not B\", and spot where an argument leans on the wrong reading","Choose between the conditional shapes of lesson 3 and the Venn check when you meet a deductive argument"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"05-inductive-strength","title":"Inductive strength: samples, analogies, causes, and best explanations","minutes":75,"objectives":["Compare deductive validity with inductive strength, and explain why new evidence can weaken a strong argument but never a valid one","Evaluate a generalisation from a sample by its size and representativeness, and an argument from analogy by the relevance of its similarities","Apply Mill's method of difference and method of concomitant variation to a causal claim, and name the four readings of a correlation","Identify an inference to the best explanation and a conductive argument, and say what makes each one strong or weak"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"06-evidence-and-probability","title":"How much should the evidence move you: base rates, conjunctions, and the prosecutor's fallacy","minutes":70,"objectives":["Calculate the share of positive results that are true positives from a base rate, a sensitivity, and a false-positive rate, by counting in natural frequencies","Explain why a conjunction can never be more probable than either of its parts, and why the Linda problem fools people anyway","Identify the prosecutor's fallacy in a courtroom-style argument and state the comparison the fact-finder should make instead","Apply the likelihood ratio to say how much a piece of evidence should change a belief, including an argument from absence of evidence"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"07-reconstruction-and-charity","title":"Reconstruction and charity: the strongest version first","minutes":70,"objectives":["Mark the assuring, guarding, and discounting terms in a passage, and reconstruct its argument into standard form, supplying the missing premises in the form the arguer would accept and marking which premises are linked and which are convergent","Apply Toulmin's layout to an argument to find its warrant, qualifier, and rebuttal","Apply the principle of charity and Rapoport's rules to an argument you disagree with, and tell a steelman from a version nobody holds","Evaluate a reconstructed argument with the acceptability, relevance, and grounds conditions and say which condition an argument fails"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"08-fallacies","title":"Fallacies: the names, the patterns, and why the experts warn you about the names","minutes":125,"objectives":["Identify the standard informal fallacies by name and group in documented real cases, and write the two-line schema for each","Explain, for a given fallacy, the cogent argument that shares its surface form and the critical questions that tell the two apart","Apply Walton's six critical questions to a real appeal to expert opinion and say which question it fails","Explain the fallacy fallacy and Massey's asymmetry, and why together they make a fallacy name a hypothesis to test rather than a verdict"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"09-reasoning-in-the-news","title":"Bad reasoning in the news and everyday life","minutes":120,"objectives":["Evaluate a source with the four rules for sources (cite, informed, impartial, cross-check) and explain what to look for when qualified experts disagree","Convert a relative-risk headline into absolute terms and natural frequencies, and find the missing base rate, denominator or axis baseline in a claim or a chart","Identify survivorship bias in a described sample and explain what Wald's analysis of returning aircraft actually did","Apply a repeatable checklist to a news argument and identify motivated reasoning in your own reading of it"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"10-building-an-argument","title":"Building an argument that holds up","minutes":70,"objectives":["Build a short argument in standard form with acceptable premises and a link you can name as valid or strong","Build an extended one-page argument that states its strongest objection and either answers it or concedes it with a qualifier","Apply the ARG conditions and a fallacy check to your own draft and revise what fails","Explain the difference between winning an argument and being right, and why the second is what this course teaches"],"quizCount":6}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":30,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":20},{"id":"project-take-an-argument-apart","title":"Take a real argument apart and answer it","type":"project","minutes":300,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/logic-and-argument.js?v=d72bc3d5"},{"id":"how-to-learn-anything","title":"How to Learn Anything","school":"learning-and-mind","subject":"Learning & Mind","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"The research on how people actually learn, turned into a method you can use on any subject, starting with this university.","description":"Most people were never taught how to learn; they were taught subjects and left to guess at the method. A century of research in cognitive psychology has produced clear, replicated findings about what makes learning stick and what only feels like it does. This course teaches those findings and turns them into a working method: how to study, practise, remember, and get genuinely good at something. It is the first course on the Foval Core because every other course goes better once you have it.","outcomes":["Explain why what you can do today is a poor guide to what you will remember next month, and spot the fluency illusion in your own habits","Apply retrieval practice, spacing, and interleaving to a real subject, with the right gaps and the right boundaries","Use worked examples, self-explanation, and elaboration to understand genuinely hard material","Design deliberate practice for a skill, with specific goals and a feedback loop, and state honestly what the evidence shows about practice and expertise","Build a weekly study system that fits the hours you actually have","Name the common learning myths and the evidence against each"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":false,"hours":19.1,"lessons":[{"id":"01-learning-is-not-performance","title":"Learning is not performance","minutes":70,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/01-learning-is-not-performance.mp3","objectives":["Explain the difference between storage strength and retrieval strength, and why the two can come apart","Identify the fluency illusion in a described study habit and say which strength it is boosting","Predict which of two study conditions will win at five minutes and which will win at one week"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"02-how-memory-works","title":"How memory actually works","minutes":70,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/02-how-memory-works.mp3","objectives":["Explain why the limits of working memory make a novice and an expert see different things in the same material","Apply the idea of chunking to a subject you are learning, by listing the chunks on a page and marking which ones you already own","Identify extraneous cognitive load in a piece of instruction and rewrite it to remove that load"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"03-retrieval-practice","title":"Retrieval practice: one of the two techniques that hold up","minutes":60,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/03-retrieval-practice.mp3","objectives":["Explain why retrieving information beats restudying it, and what \"the testing effect\" means","Design a retrieval routine for a subject you are actually studying","Distinguish self-testing to check what you know from self-testing to learn, and say what each one changes about what you do next"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"04-spacing","title":"Spacing: when to come back","minutes":65,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/04-spacing.mp3","objectives":["Calculate a sensible review gap from how long you need to remember something","Explain why the same study time spread over gaps beats the same time in one block","Set up a spaced review schedule for a real goal, including one measured in years"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"05-interleaving","title":"Interleaving and variation: mix it up, within limits","minutes":70,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/05-interleaving.mp3","objectives":["Explain what interleaved practice forces you to do on every problem that blocked practice lets you skip","Decide, for a given subject and material, whether interleaving is likely to help, do nothing, or hurt, using confusability as the first test","Design a shuffled practice set from two or three confusable things you are learning, without labels that give the choice away"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"06-understanding-hard-material","title":"Understanding hard material","minutes":50,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/06-understanding-hard-material.mp3","objectives":["Use worked examples correctly on a new type of problem (study, then attempt, then fade the guidance)","Apply self-explanation and elaborative interrogation to a dense passage and identify the gaps they expose","Recognise when guidance should be reduced as your own expertise grows, and explain why","State what is settled and what is open in the guided-versus-discovery dispute, and which part this lesson relies on"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"07-deliberate-practice","title":"Getting good at a skill: deliberate practice and feedback","minutes":60,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/07-deliberate-practice.mp3","objectives":["Define deliberate practice and distinguish it from naive practice in a described routine","Design a practice session for a real skill with a specific sub-skill, a drill, a feedback source, and a length","State what the research does and does not show about how much of the difference between performers practice explains, and why the question is unresolved"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"08-running-yourself","title":"Running yourself: attention, procrastination, sleep, and your system","minutes":70,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/learning-and-mind/how-to-learn-anything/08-running-yourself.mp3","objectives":["Build a weekly study plan that spaces and interleaves within the hours you actually have","Apply one evidence-based attention or procrastination tactic to a real study block","Explain what the evidence does and does not show about sleep and learning","Name the common learning myths, distinguish them from contested questions such as growth mindset, and give the evidence against each"],"quizCount":6}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":30,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":20},{"id":"project-two-weeks","title":"Apply the method for two weeks","type":"project","minutes":600,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/how-to-learn-anything.js?v=548230fe"},{"id":"algebra-essentials","title":"Algebra Essentials","school":"mathematics","subject":"Mathematics","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"Variables, equations, and graphs from the ground up. The algebra you need for science, finance, and further maths.","description":"Algebra is the language for describing relationships between quantities. If arithmetic is about specific numbers, algebra is about patterns that hold for any number. This course builds it up carefully: what a variable is, how to solve equations by keeping them balanced, how straight lines encode rates of change, how to turn a sentence into an equation, and how to handle two unknowns at once. It deliberately stops short of quadratics, factoring and exponents, and points you at OpenStax for those.","outcomes":["Say what a letter in an equation stands for, and evaluate an expression for a given value","Read the equals sign as a claim about two sides being the same size, and keep an equation true","Solve a linear equation step by step, justify each move, and check the answer by substitution","Read a straight line for its starting value and its rate, in the situation's own units","Turn a described relationship into an equation, and test the translation with numbers","Solve two equations at once by substitution and by elimination"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":false,"hours":7.3,"lessons":[{"id":"01-variables","title":"What a letter is","minutes":35,"objectives":["Say what a letter in algebra stands for, and what it does not stand for","Evaluate an expression by substituting a number and doing the arithmetic in the right order","Write an expression for a quantity described in words, and check it by putting a number in"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"02-equals","title":"Equals means the same size","minutes":35,"objectives":["Say what the equals sign claims, and test whether an equation is true for a given number","Keep an equation true by doing the same thing to both sides","Find a missing number in an equation with operations on both sides"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"03-equations","title":"Solving, and why the moves are legal","minutes":45,"objectives":["Solve a linear equation in one unknown, and justify each step by what it does to both sides","Handle brackets, an unknown on both sides, and a negative term without losing the sign","Check every solution by substituting it back into the original equation"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"04-lines","title":"A picture of a relationship","minutes":45,"objectives":["Read a straight line for its starting value and its rate, and say what each one means in the situation","Write the equation of a line from a described situation, and build its table","Explain what makes a relationship straight, and what a slope means in the situation's own units"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"05-translating","title":"Turning a sentence into an equation","minutes":75,"objectives":["Turn a described relationship into an equation, naming each letter as a number rather than as a thing","Test a translation by putting numbers into it, and repair it when it fails","Recognise the reversal error in your own work and say what produces it"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"06-systems","title":"Two unknowns at once","minutes":70,"objectives":["Solve a pair of linear equations by substitution and by elimination, and check the answer in both","Say what the solution of a pair means on a graph, and what parallel or identical lines look like in the algebra","Set up a pair of equations from a situation with two unknown quantities"],"quizCount":7}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":40,"pass_mark":0.7,"quizCount":18},{"id":"project-your-own-rate","title":"An equation for something in your own life","type":"project","minutes":90,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/algebra-essentials.js?v=9dd3b188"},{"id":"personal-finance","title":"Personal Finance Fundamentals","school":"money","subject":"Money","level":"Foundation","status":"published","summary":"Budgeting, saving, debt, and investing explained plainly, so money stops being a source of stress.","description":"Most people are never taught how money works. This course covers the handful of ideas that make the biggest difference: where your money goes, how to build a cushion, how debt really costs you, how compounding works for you, and what the evidence says about investing. It is general education, not personalised advice.","outcomes":["Audit a month of your own spending and judge a money statistic by what it measures","Build a budget that survives real life, including irregular income and irregular bills","Calculate what debt costs, and choose a payoff order using both the arithmetic and the evidence","Explain compounding, estimate doubling times, and separate real returns from nominal ones","Compare funds by cost and diversification, and say why the record favours low-cost indexing","Work out what a salary actually keeps after tax, and tell a marginal rate from an effective one","Compare taxable, traditional and Roth accounts, read your own plan's match and vesting terms, and order cushion, match and debt","Decide which risks to insure and which to carry, and compare two policies in a quiet year and a bad one","Cost owning a specific home against renting one, and find the holding period at which buying wins","Recognise the three things every scam has to do, rank payment methods by reversibility, and freeze your credit"],"prerequisites":[],"sensitive_domain":true,"hours":16.6,"lessons":[{"id":"01-where-money-goes","title":"Where your money actually goes","minutes":45,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/money/personal-finance/01-where-money-goes.mp3","objectives":["Categorise a month of your own transactions into buckets and compare each bucket to your take-home pay","Distinguish fixed from variable costs and identify which kind of fix a tight budget needs","Judge a money statistic or rule of thumb by what it actually measures, using the $400 factoid and 50/30/20 as cases"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"02-budget","title":"A budget that survives real life","minutes":50,"audio":"https://pub-f7bdc2ace9904917a8238f1557b7f247.r2.dev/money/personal-finance/02-budget.mp3","objectives":["Build a monthly budget from your own audit numbers, with every dollar assigned and a line for irregular costs","Choose among 50/30/20, zero-based, pay-yourself-first, and envelope budgeting by matching each method to the person it suits","Adapt a budget to irregular income using a baseline salary and a buffer"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"03-debt","title":"How debt really works","minutes":55,"objectives":["Calculate what a month of carrying a card balance costs and explain why minimum payments barely move the balance","Distinguish secured from unsecured debt and apply that distinction to consolidation offers and payoff priority","Choose a payoff order using both the arithmetic and the behavioural evidence, and place debt payoff against the cushion and an employer match"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"04-compounding","title":"Compounding: interest working for you","minutes":40,"objectives":["Explain why compound growth accelerates, using the fact that each year's gain is proportional to the current balance","Estimate doubling times with the rule of 72 and state where the rule's accuracy drifts","Convert a nominal return to an approximate real return, and judge a projected-return figure against the sourced historical record"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"05-investing","title":"Investing without the noise","minutes":45,"objectives":["Explain what a share, a bond, and a fund each give their owner, and why shedding single-company risk costs nothing in expected return","Judge an active fund's pitch against the SPIVA record, the persistence evidence, and Sharpe's cost arithmetic","Compute what an expense ratio costs over decades, and order a first investing dollar among employer match, debt, and cushion"],"quizCount":5},{"id":"06-taxes","title":"What you actually keep","minutes":55,"objectives":["Calculate the federal income tax on a given salary using marginal brackets, and explain why a raise into a higher bracket never lowers take-home pay","Distinguish a marginal rate from an effective rate and say which practical question each one answers","Compare what a deduction and a credit of the same size are worth, and explain who each is worth nothing to","Explain what withholding is, and weigh the cost of over-withholding against the cost of under-withholding"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"07-accounts","title":"Where the money lives","minutes":90,"objectives":["Explain what a tax wrapper is, and compare a taxable account, a traditional account and a Roth account by what each one taxes and when","Calculate the return on an employer match, and read a plan's match formula and vesting schedule to work out what an unvested match is worth in expectation","Apply an order of operations across cushion, match, high-rate debt and retirement contributions, and defend the order you chose, including the cases where the match should wait"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"08-insurance","title":"Handing off what you cannot absorb","minutes":95,"objectives":["Explain risk pooling, and say why an insurer expecting to profit does not make buying insurance a mistake for the insured","Sort your own risks into ones to insure and ones to carry, using the question of what you could absorb without the plan collapsing","Compare two policies by premium, deductible and out-of-pocket maximum, and total the cost of each in a quiet year and a bad one","Given two findings that disagree, state what each one counts, and say why both can be right"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"09-renting-versus-buying","title":"Renting against buying, and how to tell","minutes":115,"objectives":["Build the full cost of owning a specific home for a year, including the costs that never appear on a mortgage statement","Compare that against rent for an equivalent home and calculate the break-even holding period","Say which assumption the answer is most sensitive to, and identify the non-financial factors that can override the arithmetic entirely","Read a housing statistic, a long-run return or a net worth gap, and say what it can and cannot settle"],"quizCount":6},{"id":"10-scams-and-fraud","title":"Defending it","minutes":120,"objectives":["Identify the three things nearly every scam has to do, and find them in a message you have actually received","Rank common payment methods by how reversible they are, and explain why the method a stranger asks for is the strongest single signal","Place a credit freeze, read your own credit report, and say what to do in the first hour after a compromise","State what the FTC data shows about age and fraud, and explain why either figure on its own misleads"],"quizCount":6}],"assessments":[{"id":"final-test","title":"Final test","type":"test","minutes":45,"pass_mark":0.7,"quizCount":24},{"id":"project-one-page-plan","title":"Your own one-page financial plan","type":"project","minutes":240,"pass_mark":0.8,"quizCount":0}],"content":"data/content/personal-finance.js?v=7258a10d"}];
